Spatial patterns in 2015
Although the number of schools and net enrolment rates have
increased in the Lao POR over the past decade , many c hildren are
still not attending school reg ularly. Map 06.1 shows the distribution of
OOSC between 6and 11 years old who are supposed to attend primary
school.
Large numbers of prim ary school aged children do not atte nd school
in many villages in the northwest and the north, as well as in the eastern
parts of the south, indicated with red dots on Map 06 .1. These more
remote rural areas are often populated with peop le of non-Lao
ethno-linguistic groups. Among them , the Sino -Tibetan populations
have the highest rates of OOSCs , followed by Mon-Khmer and
Hmon g-Mien populations (see Map F2). In terms of abso lute numbers
ofOOSC, in Savannakhet, Saravane, and Champasak Provinces , many
larger villages have high rates and large numbers of OOSC.
Map 06 .3 revea ls a gender gap among the 6to11 year old OOSCs,
where in more access ible lowland areas a higher portion of OOSCs
tend to be boys, and in the poorer areas (see Map 11.1) particularly in
the southeast and the north of the Lao POR generally more OOSCs
are g irls, largely reflecting the pattern of the gende r gap in primary
education (Map 0 5.1).
